{"id":5898,"date":"2022-03-02T13:37:13","date_gmt":"2022-03-02T21:37:13","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=5898"},"modified":"2025-10-23T13:36:30","modified_gmt":"2025-10-23T20:36:30","slug":"3-1-sensors-introduction-cde-blockly","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-1-sensors-introduction-cde-blockly\/","title":{"rendered":"3.1: Sensors Introduction"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22136e8aa28\" name=\"6a22136e8aa28\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>We've Moved!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1761251786960{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-11766\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"81\" height=\"82\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>We\u2019ve moved to our new lesson portal: <a href=\"http:\/\/learn.robolink.com\">Robolink Learn<\/a>! Lessons here will remain available until the end of June 2026. This version of Basecamp will no longer be updated and uses outdated references to Blockly for Robolink. You can access the previous version of <a href=\"https:\/\/codrone.robolink.com\/edu\/blockly-archive\/\">Blockly here<\/a>.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22136e8ab8a\" name=\"6a22136e8ab8a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify the importance of sensors to drone flight<\/li>\n<li>Identify different types of sensors and their uses<\/li>\n<li>Explain why calibration of sensors is important<\/li>\n<li>Describe the types of functions that sensors use<\/li>\n<li>Explain why we use objects in programs to represent sensors<\/li>\n<li>Identify different measurements that sensor will take based on the sensor type<\/li>\n<li>Write a program to do a pre-flight check of all sensors prior to takeoff<\/li>\n<li>Identify sensors used on drones and robots in industry and their purpose<\/li>\n<li>Explain what an object is in programming and why they are useful<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>What is a Sensor?<\/li>\n<li>Sensor Values<\/li>\n<li>Sensors as Objects<\/li>\n<li>Pre-Flight Sensor Check<\/li>\n<li>Challenge: Sensor Research<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22136e8ac2d\" name=\"6a22136e8ac2d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What is a Sensor?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<strong>Sensors\u00a0<\/strong>are electronic devices that turn information about their environment into a value that can be used in a program. Like many other input devices, sensors send digital signals to a computer, which it can then use to make decisions. They are often used in electronic devices to make sense of the outside world because they are very good at turning <strong>Analog\u00a0<\/strong>information into\u00a0<strong>Digital\u00a0<\/strong>information. Analog information or data is simply data that has constantly changing values.<\/p>\n<p>Take a moment and think about a cell phone. <em>In what ways do you interact with it?<\/em> <em>What tools or apps make it useful to you?<\/em> Based on your responses, make a list of the types of sensors your phone may have.<\/p>\n<p>Once you have completed your list, see if you have any of the following listed:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>GPS<\/li>\n<li>Gyroscope (for screen rotation and phone movement)<\/li>\n<li>Light Sensor<\/li>\n<li>Camera<\/li>\n<li>Microphone<\/li>\n<li>Touch Screen<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>These sensors make our phones valuable to their users because they become more interactive and intuitive (they understand their environment). As we write programs with our CoDrone <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">EDU<\/span> to make it more interactive with its environment, there are generally four steps that we will use:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The sensor will gather environmental data (often analog)<\/li>\n<li>The sensor will convert that data into a digital value or signal<\/li>\n<li>We will store the value in a variable<\/li>\n<li>We will implement the variable into our program<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22136e8ac9f\" name=\"6a22136e8ac9f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Sensor Values<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]As we mentioned, there are a wide variety of sensors that are used in devices. However, they all send different values based on what they are measuring. Look at the list below and try to think of what values these sensors might give you:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Temperature Sensor &#8211;\u00a0<em>Answer: Degrees in Fahrenheit<\/em><\/li>\n<li>Microphone<\/li>\n<li>Camera<\/li>\n<li>Touch Screen<\/li>\n<li>Gyroscope<\/li>\n<li>Battery indicator<\/li>\n<li>Barometer<\/li>\n<li>Light Sensor<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22136e8ad1b\" name=\"6a22136e8ad1b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Sensors as Objects<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Since most sensors are simply reporting values, we typically see that the commands to get these values use the word\u00a0<strong><em>get<\/em><em>.<\/em><\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Objects <\/strong>are an abstraction tool used in many programming languages to group functions or components together. This can allow us to represent physical objects like a sensor in a digital program.<\/p>\n<p>Objects have functions and properties:<\/p>\n<p><strong>Getter:\u00a0<\/strong>retrieves a value stored in the object. Sensors use getter functions to report the values they are reading. The sensor stores and then reports a value when its getter function(s) are called.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Setter:\u00a0<\/strong>changes something about the object. Sensors typically use setter functions to reset or calibrate the sensor.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Properties:\u00a0<\/strong>These are not a function, but are values that describe the object, such as the type of sensor or its current state.<\/p>\n<p>One of the great benefits of objects is that they can be replicated multiple times, each with its own unique properties. This is one of the reasons we like to represent objects as sensors. If you image a very complex drone, it may have multiple sensors of the same type which help it navigate. For example, it might have 6 ultrasonic (distance-detecting) sensors that are all the same type that help it navigate an environment without running into things. <em>How do we differentiate between the different sensors?<\/em> Objects! Because objects can be replicated and still keep the same functions, we could create 6 different ultrasonic sensor objects and use the same functions to read their values, change their settings, etc&#8230;[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22136e8adc0\" name=\"6a22136e8adc0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Sensor Dashboard<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"\" data-source-line=\"84\">Now that you know a little bit about how sensors are used in other devices, you will learn about CoDrone EDU&#8217;s sensors. CoDrone EDU has the following sensors that you can request data from:<\/p>\n<ul data-source-line=\"85\">\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"85\">Gyroscope and accelerometer<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"86\">Internal temperature sensor<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"87\">Barometer<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"88\">Infrared front range sensor<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"89\">Infrared bottom range sensor<\/li>\n<li class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"90\">Optical flow position sensor\n<div class=\"absolute top-0 right-0 select-none floating-action flex flex-col items-end z-[60]\"><\/div>\n<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"91\">Color sensors<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Don&#8217;t worry right now about what these sensors measure or how they work. The following lessons will cover all of that information. For now, you are going to use the sensor dashboard to explore the sensors and the data that they return. Pair the drone to Blockly and click the &#8220;Sensors&#8221; tab on the right side.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12687&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"\" data-source-line=\"97\">The dashboard will open up, and you will be able to see most sensors responding in real-time. Move the drone around and see how the sensors respond. Notice that some sensors are not active while the drone is not on the surface.<\/p>\n<ul data-source-line=\"98\">\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"98\">If the color sensor returns unknown, you will learn how to calibrate it in a later lesson.<\/li>\n<li class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"99\">The position sensor will only update when the drone is actively flying.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12690&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22136e8ae31\" name=\"6a22136e8ae31\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Pre-Flight Sensor Check<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now you will use code to perform a pre-flight systems check. Commercial pilots will often check their various sensors to make sure they are reading correct values before taking off. Put your drone on a flat table and create the following code:[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13174&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<em>Once you have run the code, what values did you get?<\/em><\/p>\n<p>To figure out what each of these values means, do the following and then run the code above again:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Have the drone takeoff and then land.<\/li>\n<li>Turn the drone on its side.<\/li>\n<li>Put the drone on the floor.<\/li>\n<li>Have the drone takeoff, hover for 5 seconds, and then land.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>As we move through the lessons in this module, you will learn what each of these values means to our drone and how to use these sensors to make more advanced programs.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22136e8ae84\" name=\"6a22136e8ae84\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge: Sensor Research<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this challenge, takeoff and print 3 sensor values. Choose any values that you want to measure. Write down your data in a spreadsheet or table. Then, repeat the experiment. <em>What were your observations?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22136e8aed2\" name=\"6a22136e8aed2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you got an introduction to the CoDrone EDU sensors and how to visualize the data using the sensor dashboard. You also practiced printing sensor data. In the next few lessons, you will build more complex programs and learn more about each individual sensor![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;8627&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 10 We've Moved! [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1761251786960{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] We\u2019ve moved to our new lesson portal: Robolink Learn! Lessons here&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-1-sensors-introduction-cde-blockly\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4742,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3877,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1770,1793,1826,1482,853,694,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2195,2154,4328,14401,2122,2155],"class_list":["post-5898","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-advanced","lesson-tag-battery","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-sensor","module-advanced","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/5898","t